WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.170
Deadly stunts, an obsessed Beatle, and a game-changing
mission to Mars — this is the incredible

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.995
story of 2001: A Space Odyssey.

00:00:09.329 --> 00:00:14.339
Throughout his career, Stanley Kubrick clearly
enjoyed adapting novels into movies.

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:19.830
He drew on source material ranging from pop-fiction,
such as Stephen King's The Shining, to more

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.460
serious literary fare, like Vladimir Nabokov's
Lolita.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.300
Like many of Kubrick's other works, 2001 is
based on a book.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.350
Unlike the other adaptations, however, the
novelization of 2001 was written alongside

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.329
the film's production.

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:39.600
Inspired to write a movie about space travel
and all its possibilities, Kubrick decided

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.460
to work with the legendary sci-fi author Arthur
C. Clarke.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.920
The two men agreed to work together on a story
which Clarke would turn into a novel while

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.950
Kubrick made it into a movie.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.230
While both Clarke and Kubrick were creative
geniuses in their own right, they had a hard

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.270
time actually finishing the film's official
screenplay.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:03.269
According to a 1966 New Yorker profile on
the making of 2001, Kubrick estimated that

00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:08.970
he and Clarke spent "an average of four hours
a day, six days a week," writing the script.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:14.689
However, the film's release was delayed until
1968 as some of the plot's finer details had

00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:16.290
to be worked out on set.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.509
Whether or not there was a definitive method
to their madness, Kubrick, Clarke, and company

00:01:20.509 --> 00:01:24.000
wound up with one of cinema's most enduring
sci-fi stories.

00:01:24.442 --> 00:01:30.090
The narrative thrust behind 2001 is the idea
that, sooner or later, humans are going to

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.909
have a close encounter of the third kind.

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:37.950
It was a timely concept to explore, as during
the movie's early production stages in 1964,

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:43.220
a NASA space probe named the Mariner 4 was
on its way past Mars.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.960
Kubrick was so confident the discovery of
extraterrestrial life was imminent, he began

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:52.689
to fret that NASA would encounter something
on the Red Planet before his movie was finished.

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:57.670
Worried at the possibility of losing creative
license on a story about NASA pilots meeting

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:02.390
alien intelligence in deep space, Kubrick
decided to check if he could take out an insurance

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.560
policy on his own story.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.720
Somehow, Kubrick actually found a company
that would offer him insurance, and so he

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.480
bought the policy despite its galactic price
tag.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.910
While the human race is apparently yet to
meet extraterrestrials, it's safe to say that

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.250
Kubrick was most definitely a believer.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:25.230
Kubrick found his first hit directing 1960's
historical epic Spartacus.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.680
He followed that success with a black comedy
about the existential threat born from the

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.520
nuclear arms race: Dr. Strangelove.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:38.060
The nihilist classic is a hilarious yet devastating
satire that's often imitated, but has never

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.318
been truly matched.

00:02:39.318 --> 00:02:42.050
"Gentlemen, you can't fight in here, this
is the War Room!"

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:47.519
Kubrick had been inspired to make Dr. Strangelove
after researching nuclear defense policies

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:50.120
and taking them to their logical extremes.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:56.050
In his 1966 New Yorker profile on Kubrick,
Jeremy Bernstein quotes the director as saying:

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:01.830
"The nuclear strategists who make up all those
war scenarios are never as inventive as reality,

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:06.269
and political and military leaders are never
as sophisticated as they think they are."

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:11.370
Bleak stuff for sure, but it's entirely in
line with the central thesis of Dr. Strangelove.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:16.340
However, Bernstein argued that Kubrick found
what he referred to as "limited optimism"

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.940
in the space race.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.959
Kubrick apparently felt that the opportunities
presented by space exploration and alien contact

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:25.790
just might save the human species from itself.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:30.310
His belief in the power of space exploration
is all over 2001.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:35.540
From its beautiful soundtrack to its depiction
of man's divorce from earthbound technology,

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:39.435
Kubrick clearly thought that mankind's ventures
into space were full of potential.

00:03:40.093 --> 00:03:43.090
Depicting the future on screen is no easy
feat.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:47.909
In order to create a future audiences may
find plausible, Kubrick formed a think tank

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:53.090
consisting of 35 artists and designers, a
special effects team more than 20 strong,

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.019
and several consulting scientific advisers.

00:03:56.019 --> 00:04:01.049
This platoon of forward thinkers worked to
ensure the designs of everything felt futuristic

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:02.379
in a believable way.

00:04:02.379 --> 00:04:05.379
By most accounts,It's fair to say they succeeded.

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:09.770
The film's aesthetic is stunning, and some
of the choices made by Kubrick and his design

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:12.230
crew are still popular to this day.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:17.459
Take, for example, the movie's use of Arne
Jacobson flatware, a brand made famous by

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:20.670
2001 that is still bought and sold today.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.840
Clearly, Kubrick's attention to detail paid
dividends.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.430
While many filmgoers may not remember a full,
detailed play-by-play of the movie's sprawling

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:32.990
plot, they'll likely remember the innovative
aesthetic Kubrick curated to bring his vision

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:33.702
to life.

00:04:33.702 --> 00:04:40.380
One of the most iconic designs in 2001 is
the Discovery One, the NASA exploratory vessel

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.889
headed to Jupiter in search of an alien radio
signal.

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:48.699
In the film, Kubrick handles the spacecraft's
introduction with a level of care usually

00:04:48.699 --> 00:04:50.910
reserved for human protagonists.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.470
Its grand design fills the screen as it drifts
slowly into frame.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:59.940
Then the movie shows off the ship's distinctive
interior: a centrifuge decorated with beds,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.010
work stations, a dining table, computers,
and more.

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:07.770
Kubrick and his advisers felt that long voyages
through space would require artificial gravity

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.400
to keep astronauts healthy.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:14.639
Many, including Kubrick, also agreed that
gravity could be easily created in space with

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:16.000
the aid of a centrifuge.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.497
So, the director hired an engineering company
to build one.

00:05:19.497 --> 00:05:26.217
"Inside the giant centrifuge, the technology
of spaceflight is seen at close range."

00:05:26.217 --> 00:05:32.020
The finished product could spin up to three
miles an hour, cost $300,000, and came in

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:34.380
at about 38 feet in diameter.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:39.010
The end result is not only one of the most
innovative sets ever designed, but a wonderful

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:45.020
symbol for Kubrick's ultimate vision: Like
2001 itself, the centrifuge set is large,

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.288
expensive, and very hard to forget.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:52.569
In one of the film's most thrilling sequences,
Dave Bowman finds himself locked outside of

00:05:52.569 --> 00:05:55.880
the Discovery One in a small maintenance pod.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.930
Bowman considers leaving his pod, spacewalking
to the main ship, and climbing back inside,

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:02.330
but he doesn't have his helmet.

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:06.690
To get back into the ship without suffocating
in space, Bowman fires himself through the

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.180
pod's escape hatch into an open airlock.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.500
It's an ingenious scene that only runs about
90 seconds in total.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:18.509
In fact, the shot of Bowman launching into
the open airlock is even shorter — but that

00:06:18.509 --> 00:06:20.930
single shot was extremely dangerous.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:26.729
Actor Keir Dullea had to dive headfirst through
breakaway boards from a platform two stories

00:06:26.729 --> 00:06:32.330
above the airlock set, falling down toward
an upward-tilted camera to convincingly create

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.449
the illusion that he was launching through
weightless space.

00:06:35.449 --> 00:06:39.780
For safety, he was secured with a single rope
connected to a pulley.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:43.830
An enormous film camera was positioned at
the bottom of the airlock set to capture the

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:45.250
image in a single take.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:49.210
If something had gone wrong, Dullea would
have crashed directly into the camera — and

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:50.819
likely been killed in the process.

00:06:50.819 --> 00:06:52.940
Dullea later told Vanity Fair:

00:06:58.822 --> 00:07:03.972
The "Dawn of Man" sequence that kicks off
2001 may be one of the boldest openings ever

00:07:03.972 --> 00:07:06.137
depicted in a science fiction movie.

00:07:06.137 --> 00:07:12.030
In a wordless, ten-minute sequence, Kubrick
and company recreate primordial Earth to present

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:16.919
the concept of extraterrestrial beings jumpstarting
the evolution from ape to human.

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:22.030
Famously, the end of the sequence sees an
ape tossing a bone into the air, which slowly

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:25.270
transforms into a satellite orbiting earth.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:29.639
Kubrick originally tried to film the sequence
on location in Africa, but eventually wound

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:34.690
up creating the set in a soundstage in Britain
with a technique known as front projection.

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:39.220
The burgeoning camera system allowed creatives
to project photographic vistas directly into

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:40.220
their shot.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:44.400
However, new cameras weren't the only trick
up Kubrick's sleeve; the design team were

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.910
also tasked with designing costumes for the
apes.

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:52.159
Despite their best efforts, the sequence went
largely ignored come awards season.

00:07:52.159 --> 00:07:56.520
Somewhat ironically, the Best Costume Design
Oscar went to Planet of the Apes.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.040
"Damn you!

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.693
God damn you all to hell!"

00:08:03.693 --> 00:08:08.919
While 2001 became the biggest box office earner
of the year when it was released in 1968,

00:08:08.919 --> 00:08:14.330
MGM took quite a leap of faith when they originally
decided to back Kubrick's project.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:19.800
Kubrick first sent MGM Arthur Clarke's 250
page draft narrative, rather than a finished

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:25.549
screenplay, and yet studio President Robert
O'Brien still agreed to back the filmmaker's vision.

00:08:25.549 --> 00:08:30.190
Given the film's many delays, it's safe to
assume O'Brien's confidence may have been

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:31.476
tested along the way.

00:08:31.476 --> 00:08:36.779
MGM's original press release for the film
claimed that 2001 was to be ready for release

00:08:36.779 --> 00:08:38.610
by 1966.

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:45.130
The movie was delayed until 1968, and in '66
the movie's budget increased from $6 million

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:46.890
to $7 million.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:52.880
In the end, the film cost MGM around $10 million,
almost double its initial budget.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:57.120
Even though the film eventually found success
over the course of its release year, the saga

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.209
concluded with a shareholder revolt.

00:08:59.209 --> 00:09:05.089
According to The New York Times, MGM was also
making another expensive film, Dr. Zhivago,

00:09:05.089 --> 00:09:06.420
around the same time.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:11.329
With the company facing such a financially
uncertain future, O'Brien wound up resigning

00:09:11.329 --> 00:09:13.456
as president in 1969.

00:09:13.913 --> 00:09:19.300
Even though 2001 is widely regarded as a stone
cold classic today, initial reactions from

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.329
movie critics were not particularly kind.

00:09:22.329 --> 00:09:27.130
Legendary movie critic Pauline Kael said the
film was "ponderous" and "blurry" in her review

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:28.339
for Harper's.

00:09:28.339 --> 00:09:33.460
In The New York Times, critic Renata Adler
said the movie's "uncompromising slowness"

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:35.800
rendered the film "hard to sit through without
talking."

00:09:36.487 --> 00:09:39.031
"Well, I don't think there is any question
about it.

00:09:40.126 --> 00:09:43.529
It can only be attributable to human error."

00:09:43.529 --> 00:09:46.589
Kubrick was clearly bothered by the early
negative press.

00:09:46.589 --> 00:09:51.910
In a 1968 interview with Playboy, the director
expressed his annoyance, insisting that only

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:54.670
New York had really been hostile to the movie.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:59.560
He went on to blame the sensibilities of a
cohort he referred to as New York City's "lumpen

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:05.119
literati" for their own aversion to his movie's
sincere expression of grandeur and cosmic wonder.

00:10:05.119 --> 00:10:10.600
While 2001 certainly marches to its own deliberate
pace, it's safe to say that the movie has

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:13.100
aged much better than its initial reviews.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:18.490
Today, for example, it holds a top 20 spot
on the American Film Institute's "100 Greatest

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:20.690
American Films of All Time" list.

00:10:20.975 --> 00:10:26.180
2001 may not have been an immediate hit with
the critics, but it very quickly gained cult

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:27.399
status with hippies.

00:10:27.399 --> 00:10:32.630
In fact, the movie was so popular with pillars
of the '60s counter culture community that

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:37.829
The New Yorker's 50th anniversary retrospective
argues that it may have been a hippie audience

00:10:37.829 --> 00:10:39.950
that saved the film from obscurity.

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:45.370
Supposedly, fans of the film consumed psychedelic
drugs in sync with Dave Bowman's trip through

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:49.210
the colorful, chaotic wormhole at the climax
of the film.

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:54.339
MGM's marketing gurus eventually got wind
of the phenomenon and added a new tagline

00:10:54.339 --> 00:10:56.620
to posters: "The Ultimate Trip."

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:02.100
But word of mouth and "ultimate trips" weren't
the only things bolstering the film's popularity.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:06.870
It also received endorsement from rock legends
such as David Bowie and John Lennon.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:11.360
Lennon, specifically, liked it so much that
he claimed to have seen it every week during

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.037
its initial theatrical run.

00:11:13.177 --> 00:11:19.050
2001 didn't just inspire a generation of filmgoers
to look up to the stars and wonder about the

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:23.279
future — it also inspired tons of today's
best filmmakers.

00:11:23.279 --> 00:11:28.060
For the movie's 50th anniversary, IndieWire
collected thoughts from a collection of famous

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:32.210
filmmakers on Kubrick's master entry to the
sci-fi canon.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:37.459
The list featured praise from directors including
Steven Spielberg, Claire Denis, Christopher

00:11:37.459 --> 00:11:42.560
Nolan, James Cameron, George Lucas, Ridley
Scott, and Alfonso Cuarón.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:47.230
Film fans may not be particularly surprised
by this collection of acolytes, as Kubrick's

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:50.230
lasting impact on the industry cannot be undersold.

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:55.740
However, the impact of 2001 lives on outside
of the elite of blockbuster filmmakers.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:12:01.269
A 50th anniversary retrospective in Vulture
credits the film for opening up possibilities

00:12:01.269 --> 00:12:04.529
for headier, more complex sci-fi in the indie
realm.

00:12:04.529 --> 00:12:10.470
On top of that, any sci-fi movie about nefarious
artificial intelligence made since 1968 owes

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:14.905
a serious debt to the original murderous computer,
HAL 9000.

00:12:15.098 --> 00:12:16.603
"Open the pod bay doors, Hal."

00:12:18.234 --> 00:12:19.733
"I'm sorry, Dave.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.026
I'm afraid I can't do that."

00:12:22.124 --> 00:12:27.290
Clearly, Kubrick went on an odyssey of his
own to get 2001 made and perfect his vision.

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:32.931
In the end, though, he introduced audiences
to whole new cosmic possibilities — at the

00:12:32.931 --> 00:12:34.279
movies and beyond.

